# Deploy

This is the last page, and it answers the question that has been hanging over
every unit you built: **where does all of this actually run, and how does it
ship?** The Sales workbook is one folder of `.work` files. We never rewrote it
for a phone, a CDN, or a server farm. We authored it once. Here we show how that
one authoring model becomes a thing you can hand to a stranger.

There are two ideas to keep separate, and most of the confusion in deployment
comes from conflating them:

- **Targets** — *where the nexus lives*. There are exactly two: **local** and
  **cloud**. One OCI image, two homes.
- **Delivery modes** — *how the page reaches a browser*. There are three:
  **static bundle**, **nexus SSR**, and **live SSR**. Same workbook, no rewrite.

Everything here references the units we already built — [[Pipeline]] orchestrates
[[Enrich]] and [[Score]], the [[Plan]] board holds the team's work, and the
client islands [[Chart]] and [[Sparkline]] hydrate in the browser. Deploy is
just the act of placing all of that somewhere a person can reach it.

## The weave is the build

`work bundle` is the compiler. It walks this folder tree top to bottom, resolves
every `[[backlink]]`, `:atom`, and `@type` reference into a code-graph edge,
audits that every `grant:` a unit asked for is one the Dock can fulfill, compiles
each foreign-language unit to a wasm component, and runs the tests. The output is
a single self-contained gzipped HTML file: `salesapp.html`.

The gate is not advisory. A red test ships **no bundle** — the weave refuses to
emit a file it could not prove. So the build is also the audit.

server compute :weave do
  defmodule Result do
    defstruct bundle: nil, units: 0, edges: 0, caps_ok: false, tests: :unknown
  end

  # The weave is a pipeline of gates. Each returns {:ok, ...} or {:error, reason};
  # the first error stops the build, and no bundle file is written.
  def build(tree) do
    with {:ok, graph}  <- resolve_refs(tree),
         {:ok, caps}   <- audit_caps(graph),
         {:ok, units}  <- compile_units(graph),
         {:ok, :green} <- run_tests(units) do
      html = emit_html(units, graph)
      {:ok, %Result{
        bundle: html,
        units: length(units),
        edges: graph.edge_count,
        caps_ok: caps == :granted,
        tests: :green
      }}
    else
      {:error, reason} -> {:error, reason}
    end
  end

  # A failing test is the build failing — not a warning printed beside a shipped
  # file. The contract is: green or nothing.
  defp run_tests(units) do
    case Enum.all?(units, & &1.passed) do
      true  -> {:ok, :green}
      false -> {:error, :tests_red}
    end
  end
end

## Two targets, one image

A target is **where the nexus lives**, and the locked model gives us exactly two:
**local** and **cloud**. The crucial thing — the thing that makes this not a
fork — is that *both run the same OCI image*. Local is that image in a Linux
container on your machine (krunvm on a Mac, behind a `krunvm | podman | docker`
seam). Cloud is that same image on a Machine that scales to zero. You do not
build a "dev server" and a separate "prod server." You build one runtime and
choose its home.

That single image carries the whole nexus job: native Elixir on the BEAM running
[[Pipeline]] and the [[Plan]] board, the wasmtime sandboxes hosting [[Forecast]]
(Rust + Zig) and [[Risk]] (Python), the agent loops behind [[Analyst]], and the
Dock membrane that fulfills every `grant:`.

server compute :targets do
  # A target is structure, not a DSL. The fields fall out of their defaults —
  # `where` is one of two atoms, `image` is the one image both share.
  defmodule Target do
    defstruct where: :local, image: "ghcr.io/workbooks-sh/runtime:latest", scale_to_zero: false
  end

  @targets [:local, :cloud]

  # Local and cloud differ only in placement and lifecycle, never in code. The
  # image string is identical; that identity is the whole point.
  def local,
    do: %Target{where: :local, scale_to_zero: false}

  def cloud,
    do: %Target{where: :cloud, scale_to_zero: true}

  def valid?(%Target{where: w}) when w in @targets, do: true
  def valid?(%Target{}), do: false

  # `work deploy` reads this and places the same image at the chosen home. It is
  # the user's tool — it runs the runtime image; it is never how the platform
  # itself publishes that image.
  def deploy(%Target{} = t) do
    case valid?(t) do
      true  -> {:ok, {:deployed, t.where, t.image}}
      false -> {:error, :unknown_target}
    end
  end
end

## Three delivery modes, no rewrite

A *target* is where the nexus lives. A *delivery mode* is how a page reaches a
browser. The same Sales workbook ships three ways with no change to the source —
because placement (`client` / `server`) is already declared on every unit, the
delivery mode just decides *who renders the server regions and how the diff
travels*.

server compute :delivery do
  defmodule Mode do
    defstruct name: :static, renders_server_regions: :browser, transport: :file
  end

  @modes [:static, :nexus_ssr, :live_ssr]

  # (a) Static bundle. There is no nexus in the loop at all. `salesapp.html` is a
  #     file you can email; the BEAM-placed regions were rendered once at weave
  #     time and frozen into the page, and the client islands ([[Chart]],
  #     [[Sparkline]]) hydrate in the browser. This is the floor: a workbook is
  #     HTML, so the browser renders it everywhere.
  def static,
    do: %Mode{name: :static, renders_server_regions: :weave_time, transport: :file}

  # (b) Nexus SSR. A target is live. The server regions — [[Pipeline]], the
  #     [[Plan]] board, anything `server` — render on the BEAM per request; the
  #     `client` units arrive as islands and hydrate. You get fresh server data
  #     without giving up the island model.
  def nexus_ssr,
    do: %Mode{name: :nexus_ssr, renders_server_regions: :beam_per_request, transport: :http}

  # (c) Live SSR. Same server rendering, but instead of a fresh page the nexus
  #     streams *diffs* over the RCP websocket. The [[Plan]] board mutates in
  #     place as agents claim tasks; live state, declarative document, one wire.
  def live_ssr,
    do: %Mode{name: :live_ssr, renders_server_regions: :beam_per_request, transport: :rcp_ws}

  def modes, do: @modes

  # The mode is a runtime choice, not an authoring one. The same units, the same
  # grants, the same code graph — only the renderer and the transport change.
  def pick(name) when name in @modes do
    %{static: &static/0, nexus_ssr: &nexus_ssr/0, live_ssr: &live_ssr/0}
    |> Map.fetch!(name)
    |> apply([])
  end
end

The matrix is the whole story, and it is small. Pick a **target** for where the
runtime lives; pick a **mode** for how the page arrives. A static bundle needs no
target at all — it is a file. The two SSR modes need a live target, local or
cloud, and differ only in whether the browser gets a fresh page or a stream of
diffs.

| mode | server regions render | transport | needs a target |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `:static` | once, at weave time | a `.html` file | no |
| `:nexus_ssr` | on the BEAM, per request | HTTP | yes |
| `:live_ssr` | on the BEAM, per request | RCP websocket | yes |

## Ship it

Deploy is the last gate composed from the rest: weave the tree, pick a target,
pick a mode, place the image. If the weave is red, nothing leaves the building.

server flow :ship do
  step :weave do
    :weave.build(workspace_tree())
  end

  # Choose where the nexus lives. Local for a teammate's laptop, cloud for the
  # world; both run the one image from `:targets`.
  step :place, after: :weave do
    :targets.cloud() |> :targets.deploy()
  end

  # Choose how it reaches a browser. Here we serve the live board so the [[Plan]]
  # page mutates as agents work; a static export is one mode-swap away.
  step :serve, after: :place do
    :delivery.pick(:live_ssr)
  end

  done do
    {:shipped, "salesapp.html"}
  end
end

That is the entire model. One folder of HTML, woven by `work bundle` into one
file. Two places it can live — `local` and `cloud`, the same image. Three ways it
can reach a reader — a frozen file, a server-rendered page, a live stream. You
learned the format by reading a real app; now you ship the real app. Back to
[[Welcome]] to read it again with the whole picture in view.
